In-progress minimization (no longer depends on futures or crates other than `std`, still depends on coroutines tho):

```rs
#![feature(coroutines, stmt_expr_attributes, yield_expr)]

trait TraitA { fn m(&self); }
impl<G: Send> TraitA for G { fn m(&self) {} }

trait TraitB { type Ty: Send; }

impl<F, T> TraitB for F
where
    F: for<'a> Fn(&'a (dyn TraitC + 'static)) -> T,
    T: Send,
{
    type Ty = T;
}

trait TraitC {}

fn main() {
    let x: &dyn TraitA = &#[coroutine] || {
        let _x = g(|_: &_| {});
        yield ();
    };
    x.m()
}

fn g<T: TraitB>(_: T) -> impl Send {
    (loop {}) as T::Ty
}
```

A different minimization

```rust
trait TaskFn {
    fn call_task(&self);
}

impl<Fut: Send> TaskFn for Fut {
    fn call_task(&self) {}
}

trait Visit {
    type Unit: Send;
}

impl Visit for for<'a> fn(&'a &'static i32) {
    type Unit = ();
}

fn make_none<V: Visit>() -> Option<impl Send> {
    None::<V::Unit>
}

fn main() {
    let _ = (&async {
        let _x = make_none::<for<'a> fn(&'a &'static i32)>();
        std::future::pending::<()>().await;
    } as &dyn TaskFn)
        .call_task();
}
```

Relatedly, this code doesn't compile. (Note that `make_none` now returns an `impl Sized` instead of `impl Send`.)

I think this ought to compile, since auto traits are supposed to work through `impl Trait` opaque types.

```rust
trait Visit {
    type Unit: Send;
}

impl Visit for for<'a> fn(&'a &'static i32) {
    type Unit = ();
}

fn make_none<V: Visit>() -> Option<impl Sized> {
    None::<V::Unit>
}

fn foo() -> impl Send {
    async {
        let _x = make_none::<for<'a> fn(&'a &'static i32)>();
        std::future::pending::<()>().await;
    }
}
```

```
error: implementation of `Visit` is not general enough
  --> src/lib.rs:14:5
   |
14 | /     async {
15 | |         let _x = make_none::<for<'a> fn(&'a &'static i32)>();
16 | |         std::future::pending::<()>().await;
17 | |     }
   | |_____^ implementation of `Visit` is not general enough
   |
   = note: `for<'a> fn(&'a &'0 i32)` must implement `Visit`, for any lifetime `'0`...
   = note: ...but `Visit` is actually implemented for the type `for<'a> fn(&'a &'static i32)`
```

That's a fun one. We fixed the unsoundness by reverting https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/pull/152287. However, that PR was reasonable and should not have caused an unsoundness, so what's going on here.

The core issue is the interation of https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/110338 with opaque types. When computing the witness type for coroutines we do not have region information available, so every occurance of a region is replaced with a unique bound region.
```rust
trait Trait {
    type Assoc;
}
impl Trait for &'static u32 {
    type Assoc = ();
}
struct W<T: Trait>(T::Assoc);

fn is_send<T: Send>(_: T) {}
fn main() {
    // The witness contains `W<&'static u32>`. We don't know that
    // when computing the witness type and return `exists<'a> W<&'a u32>`
    // instead.
    //
    // To prove that the coroutine implements `Send` we need to show
    // `for<'a> W<&'a u32>: Send`, however, `'!a != 'static`, resulting
    // in an error when normalizing the field of `W`.
    is_send(async {
        std::future::ready(W::<&'static u32>(())).await;
    });
}
```

We can "preserve" the `W<&'static u32>: Send` proof by hiding it behind an opaque type:
```rust
trait Trait {
    type Assoc;
}
impl Trait for &'static u32 {
    type Assoc = ();
}
struct W<T: Trait>(T::Assoc);

fn prove_send_and_hide<T: Send>(x: T) -> impl Send { x } 
fn is_send<T: Send>(_: T) {}
pub fn main() {
    // The witness is now `exists<'a> opaque::<W<&'a u32>>`
    // we never actually look into the opaque when proving
    // `opaque::<W<&'!a u32>>` during analysis, so this
    // coroutine does actually implement `Send`.
    is_send(async {
        let opaque_ty = prove_send_and_hide(W::<&'static u32>(()));
        std::future::ready(opaque_ty).await;
    });
}
```

However, this only works as long as we treat `impl Send` opaquely, if proving `Send` for it actually tries to look at its underlying type we get a higher-ranked region error.

We look into opaque types during codegen, so checking whether this coroutine is `Send` there will emit a `'placeholder: 'static` region constraint again: 
```rust
//@ compile-flags: -Znext-solver
//@ known-bug: trait-system-refactor-initiative#251
trait Trait {
    type Assoc;
}
impl Trait for &'static u32 {
    type Assoc = ();
}
struct W<T: Trait>(T::Assoc);

fn prove_send_and_hide<T: Send>(x: T) -> impl Send { x } 
fn as_dyn_send(_: &dyn Send) {}
pub fn main() {
    // Checking whether the cast to the trait object is correct
    // during MIR validation uses `TypingMode::PostAnalysis` and
    // therefore looks into the opaque.
    as_dyn_send(&async move {
        let opaque_ty = prove_send_and_hide(W::<&'static u32>(()));
        std::future::ready(opaque_ty).await;
    });
}
```

We do not check these region constraints outside of analysis, except in the *leak check* during trait solver. This means whether proving that the coroutine witness is `Send` fails after analysis depends on the specifics of whether we propagate region constraints and where we leak check. This differs between the old and new solver.

In the old solver, `select` does not propagate constraints from nested `Trait` goals and also only uses the `leak_check` when doing candidate winnowing. While the old solver also adds this incorrect region constraint here, it never actually checks it and successfully compiles.

The new solver does always propagate region constraints upwards, and also always uses the leak check at the end of every candidate. This means we're able to prove that the coroutine is `Send` during analysis, but not during codegen.

**This was already unsound before https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/pull/152287** as this can affect any use of `impossible_predicates`. That PR just made it easier to hit, as right now we only check this for specific vtable entries:
```rust
trait Trait {
    type Assoc;
}
impl Trait for &'static u32 {
    type Assoc = ();
}
struct W<T: Trait>(T::Assoc);

fn prove_send_and_hide<T: Send>(x: T) -> impl Send { x } 
trait ImpossiblePredicates<F> {
    fn call_me(&self)
    where 
        F: Send,
    {
        println!("method body");
    }
}
impl<F> ImpossiblePredicates<F> for () {}

fn mk_trait_object<F>(_: F) -> Box<dyn ImpossiblePredicates<F>> {
    Box::new(())
}
pub fn main() {
    let obj = mk_trait_object(async {
        let opaque_ty = prove_send_and_hide(W::<&'static u32>(()));
        std::future::ready(opaque_ty).await;
    });
    // `call_me` is not a valid entry of the vtable as we consider
    // the coroutine witness to not implement `Send` during codegen.
    // This is UB.
    obj.call_me();
}
```

One way in which this issue is harder to trigger is that the only errors during codegen are due to the leak check. As the leak check does not currently consider `TypeOutlives` constraints, replacing the self type of the `Trait` impl with `T` and `T: 'static` avoids this issue:

```rust
//@ compile-flags: -Znext-solver
//@ check-pass
trait Trait {
    type Assoc;
}
impl<T: 'static> Trait for T {
    type Assoc = ();
}
struct W<T: Trait>(T::Assoc);

fn prove_send_and_hide<T: Send>(x: T) -> impl Send { x } 
fn as_dyn_send(_: &dyn Send) {}
pub fn main() {
    // Checking whether the cast to the trait object is correct
    // during MIR validation uses `TypingMode::PostAnalysis` and
    // therefore looks into the opaque.
    as_dyn_send(&async move {
        let opaque_ty = prove_send_and_hide(W::<&'static u32>(()));
        std::future::ready(opaque_ty).await;
    });
}
```
